# jetson-vendor-stack — tell pacman the truth about this board's GPU stack.
#
# The L4T image installs libcuda, the Vulkan ICD and the tegra EGL/GBM libraries
# outside pacman's knowledge. Because no installed package claims the virtuals
# they satisfy, pacman offers desktop driver packages the moment anything asks
# for vulkan-driver — and nvidia-utils would overwrite NVIDIA's own files.
#
# This package claims only what is demonstrably true on this machine:
#   vulkan-driver            /etc/vulkan/icd.d/nvidia_icd.json + loader
#   libcuda.so=1             /usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/libcuda.so.1
#
# It deliberately does NOT claim `nvidia-utils`: if something genuinely needs
# that package, it should fail loudly (see the refuse hook) instead of being
# silently satisfied by a lie.
pkgname=jetson-vendor-stack
pkgver=39.2.1
pkgrel=1
pkgdesc="Claims the virtual providers the NVIDIA L4T image supplies outside pacman"
arch=('aarch64')
url="local"
license=('custom')
depends=()
provides=('vulkan-driver' 'libcuda.so=1')
conflicts=()
options=('!strip')

package() {
  install -dm755 "$pkgdir/usr/share/doc/$pkgname"
  cat > "$pkgdir/usr/share/doc/$pkgname/README" <<'DOC'
This package installs nothing that executes. It exists so pacman knows the
NVIDIA L4T stack on this Jetson already provides:

  vulkan-driver   /etc/vulkan/icd.d/nvidia_icd.json
  libcuda.so=1    /usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/libcuda.so.1

Without it, `pacman -S anything-with-a-gui` offers to install nvidia-utils —
the desktop driver package — which conflicts with the vendor files in
/usr/bin/nvidia-*, /usr/lib/xorg/modules/drivers/nvidia_drv.so and
/usr/share/glvnd/egl_vendor.d/10_nvidia.json and would break the GPU stack.

If a package genuinely needs nvidia-utils (libnvidia-ml, nvidia-smi), that is a
real gap on Jetson and should be solved deliberately, not by overriding files.
DOC
}
